' Any letter can be used as an array: the address B + I is element I.
' Read numbers until a negative one, then print them backwards.
' This is Grogono's program (BYTE, July 1979, p. 204), with its limit check,
' two corrections and some spacing in the output:
'   - the article reads with "B A. + . ? =", one period too many, which
'     stores at address 0 instead of in the array (a slip); it is
'     "B A. + ? =" here;
'   - the article tests "A. 25 -", which lets a 26th number in, one past Z
'     (a slip: the text says no more than 25); it is "A. 24 -" here.
"Enter numbers, ending with a negative one:!"
A 0 =
( B A. + ? =
  B A. + . 1 + ^                           ' stop on a negative number
  A A. 1 + =
  A. 24 - [ "ARRAY FULL!" 0 ^ ] )
( A. ^
  A A. 1 - =
  B A. + . ! " " )
"!"
$$
